WEBVTT

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:19.480
We live in a very weird world.

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:25.520
And the more we discover about our planet, the stranger it gets.

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:34.080
Every day, new stories reach us - stories that surprise us...

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:36.440
What is THAT?

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:37.000
'..Shock us...'

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:37.360
Whoa!

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:38.280
That is so cool.

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:40.040
Oh, my God!

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:40.960
..Sometimes even scare us.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:44.840
Oh, my God!

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:49.920
We've scoured the globe to bring you the most curious creatures, the most extraordinary people...

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:56.280
I can stick almost anything to my skin without no glue.

00:00:55.400 --> 00:01:16.320
..And the most bizarre behaviour... ..using eyewitness accounts and expert opinion to explore a weird world of unexplained underwater blobs, flying goats and glow-in-the-dark fish.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:29.520
We examine the evidence, test the theories, to work out what on earth is going on.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:37.720
In this episode, we'll be shedding light on some of nature's weirdest mysteries.

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:56.280
Why is this shark dressed for a night on the town?

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:02.280
What has so enraged this placid marine mammal?

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:06.240
And why has this guy got peanuts stuck on his head?

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:09.000
Pull.

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:17.720
Our trek into the world of weirdness kicks off with a tour of the transformative powers of light.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:27.240
This is Hessdalen - a sleepy Norwegian town.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:29.440
There are hills and mountains and, well...

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:31.200
To be honest, that's pretty much it.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:51.600
But when the sun goes down, its reputation for the weird has given this remote village global notoriety.

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:17.520
Nope, that's not a trailer for the latest Scandi crime thriller.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:35.120
This is simply what Hessdalen's night sky looks like - sometimes as often as 15-20 times every week since as far back as anyone can remember.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:04:02.640
And a good mystery draws crowds, including intrigued academic Erling and the contents of his research shed.

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:11.840
He was determined to find some answers.

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:22.920
Now, of course, the locals would love them to be UFOs, but let's just put that notion aside for a moment and explore some more earthly possibilities.

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:32.360
Elsewhere in the world, earthquakes have sparked strange lights caused by the build-up of electrical charge where the ground rips apart.

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:43.680
So how about seismic activity?

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:48.200
Scratch that off the list, then.

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:50.760
But Erling's not alone in his research.

00:04:49.920 --> 00:05:02.160
Atmospheric physicist Bjorn Gitle Hauge has also been conducting some experiments of his own.

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:07.920
He took some readings of the light emanating from the phenomenon and discovered something extraordinary.

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:22.920
It was made of the same sort of light as the sun - a huge burning ball of plasma.

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:32.320
Now, we probably all know that there are three states of matter, if we consider water.

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:46.400
But in fact, there's a fourth state of matter.

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:48.440
It's called plasma.

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:57.520
It's like a supercharged gas - a gas on steroids - a gas where the atoms have got so hot, they've split apart and they're emitting light.

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:10.160
It may sound sci-fi but, in fact, you can make plasma very simply.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:16.840
All you need is one grape, sliced in two but still connected, one microwave and 15 seconds.

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:24.880
All the energy in the microwave gets concentrated on those two tiny grape halves, bouncing from one to the other.

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:33.400
The skin between them becomes a bottleneck of energy, getting so hot that the charged atoms split apart.

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:38.560
And hey, presto!

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:44.320
Collecting at the top of the beaker - plasma.

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:50.720
The same type of light, it seems, as the sun's and Norway's strange phenomenon.

00:06:51.160 --> 00:07:01.640
Quite good but not brilliant.

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:02.840
In fact, it's got one massive flaw.

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:03.280
You see, plasma is hot.

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:14.120
In fact, plasma is very, very hot and there's one thing you must have noticed about Hessdalen - it's snowy.

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:25.280
Very snowy.

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:42.120
Not even a trace of melting snow, so not plasma after all.

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:47.080
But all is not lost.

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:56.200
The scientists have one final theory up their sleeves, and it might just hold the key.

00:07:56.440 --> 00:08:09.680
To understand it, we need to look at the valley itself.

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:29.120
The Hessdalen Valley might be acting like a giant natural battery.

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:34.000
Hessdalen Valley is divided in two, unsurprisingly, by a river.

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:39.600
To the west are the remains of old zinc mines.

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:42.520
To the east, the remains of old copper mines.

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:46.440
Think of these as the metal ends of a classic AA.

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:55.760
And, when it rains, water pours into the shaft of an old sulphur mine nearby, dissolving the sulphur as it goes.

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:06.640
It drains into the river and pollutes it with a potent acidic mix - the final ingredient of a battery.

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:11.160
Could this be what's powering Hessdalen's light show?

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:12.920
Well, it works all too well in our lab.

00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:27.440
A few simple beakers of acidic mud, each containing a stick of zinc and a bit of copper pipe, are all you need to power a light bulb.

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:35.920
So just imagine the energy a natural battery the size of an entire valley could create.

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:48.480
Well, potentially enough for a severe electric shock, as Bjorn found out one time when he took a meter reading of Hessdalen.

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:52.080
Ouch!

00:09:51.160 --> 00:10:05.160
That's the equivalent of over 600 car batteries.

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:21.920
It's an amazing thought that there's enough power in the earth beneath Hessdalen to charge the air above it, firing lights into the sky.

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:22.200
But it is just a theory.

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:22.440
It hasn't been proven yet.

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:29.920
And they still need to work out just how the battery could cause the lights to hang in the air.

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:30.960
But...

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:31.280
It's pretty compelling - got to say that.

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:36.200
And I've also got to say that, for the time being, it's the best theory they've got.

00:10:40.920 --> 00:10:51.400
Next, we're off across the globe to Yosemite National Park, US of A, where a glow of a different kind has been hogging the headlines.

00:10:50.680 --> 00:11:05.840
Just look at Yosemite's Horsetail Falls, better known as the Firefalls - a waterfall of flames.

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:10.920
What is this fiery phenomenon?

00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:17.640
Well, Michael Mariant should know.

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:21.360
After all, he's spent 20 years trying to capture it on camera.

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:26.920
The very first time you see the Firefall actually happen, it's breathtaking.

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:33.240
It really is amazing, like liquid lava flowing down the side of the falls.

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:37.480
Liquid lava - sounds like a crazy idea, doesn't it?

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:40.680
But it might just be possible.

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:47.000
You see, an age-old event in Yosemite really did light up the hillsides with real fire.

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:55.440
A hotel-keeper used to pour his dwindling campfire coals over the edge of a Yosemite cliff.

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:55.720
This accidental spectacle caught on.

00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:59.040
Soon, the park's tourists were paying good money to see it.

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:13.720
Silently, the glowing cascade spans out, drifting down in slow, majestic motions, for a fleeting moment holding its beauty and holding you spellbound.

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:23.200
Then you gather it up as your most treasured memory of Yosemite - valley of enchantment.

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:30.680
Visitors down below would look up and just see a rain of coals falling down, and they called it the Firefall.

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:39.480
But this is not our Firefall because the coal drop was banned by the Park Service in the late 1960s.

00:12:38.680 --> 00:12:43.080
And these incredible images were taken in recent years.

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:46.040
So, if it's not fire, what is it?

00:12:47.440 --> 00:12:50.480
What's truly weird is the timing.

00:12:49.720 --> 00:12:56.800
For 11 more months, the falls are...well, nothing special, really.

00:12:56.160 --> 00:13:02.640
But in late February, the paparazzi pile in.

00:13:01.840 --> 00:13:05.560
Some years, they get lucky.

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:11.120
Others, not a hint of inferno.

00:13:13.080 --> 00:13:15.000
So what's going on here?

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:22.640
I mean, sometimes it's there, sometimes it's not, only in February, but not every February.

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:27.920
Well, the answer lies way above the falls.

00:13:28.360 --> 00:13:35.040
In the depths of winter, Yosemite freezes over.

00:13:34.120 --> 00:13:45.400
But as spring appears, so does the sun, melting the snow in the mountains above to create a waterfall below.

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:49.880
Now, on lucky years, the sun starts to work its magic as early as February, giving us February falls.

00:13:54.520 --> 00:14:06.520
Well, there's plenty of water, it's a fabulous cascade, but it's simply not glowing.

00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:12.480
You see, to get the full Firefalls effect, you need light.

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:14.200
In fact, you need lots of light.

00:14:14.840 --> 00:14:18.840
The fiery glow is, in fact, the light of the setting sun.

00:14:17.920 --> 00:14:24.000
So let's see how it works.

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:46.120
As the sun lowers, the rocks around the waterfall gradually fall into shadow until, for just a moment, the sun hits the falls at such an angle that the land around it is already in shadow, and the falls glow a brilliant orange, a brilliant optical illusion.

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:55.080
And suddenly, the sun hits and it's just this glow.

00:14:54.440 --> 00:14:59.400
And it's only about a minute and a half, two minutes, that it's absolutely perfect.

00:14:58.640 --> 00:15:10.280
Only in February does the sun strike the water at this angle.

00:15:16.960 --> 00:15:21.960
This really is a unique freak of nature.

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:42.280
Just incredible, the tricks that light can play on our land, but it's nothing compared to what it can do below the tide line, which is where we're headed next - to meet a creature that single-handedly changed the face of marine science... ..a simple green eel... ..snapped in 2011 on a Caribbean coral reef.

00:15:41.320 --> 00:16:01.920
Now, it may not look much, but thanks to this one eel, we've discovered that our oceans don't look like this.

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:02.560
They look like this.

00:16:07.280 --> 00:16:16.160
Now, to understand this weirdness, we first need a crash course in how light works.

00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:25.360
Now, we all know that white light is made up of the full spectrum of colour - all the colours of the rainbow, if you like.

00:16:24.440 --> 00:16:37.720
But the reds and the oranges are made up of light with longer wavelengths, whereas the blues, the greens and the indigoes are made up of shorter wavelengths.

00:16:36.920 --> 00:16:49.040
Now, all of this light can penetrate through air, but when it comes to water, it's only these short wavelength blue lights that can penetrate any distance.

00:16:48.400 --> 00:16:53.160
And that is why the sea is blue.

00:16:53.360 --> 00:17:00.680
But as we travel deeper and deeper, that blue makes the ocean look...well, a bit boring.

00:16:59.840 --> 00:17:04.040
Imagine we're dropping underwater.

00:17:03.080 --> 00:17:08.839
As soon as we start going down, after only about ten metres, all the red is gone.

00:17:08.040 --> 00:17:16.440
So if you were to bleed underwater and you look at your blood, it would look black because there's no red light there.

00:17:15.640 --> 00:17:18.680
But, in fact, our vision is letting us down.

00:17:18.640 --> 00:17:30.280
This blue light is responsible for a whole world of colour down there that our eyes simply cannot see.

00:17:29.440 --> 00:17:39.560
Marine biologist David has long been studying corals that fluoresce.

00:17:40.400 --> 00:17:50.480
Fluorescent molecules in their skin absorb the high-energy blue light, use up a bit of the energy, and then release what's left as lower energy wavelengths.

00:17:49.520 --> 00:17:53.960
In other words, a different set of colours.

00:17:53.000 --> 00:17:59.280
Shine a powerful beam on them and suddenly, it becomes clear to us too.

00:17:58.600 --> 00:17:59.720
OK.

00:17:58.840 --> 00:18:01.200
That is already pretty weird.

00:18:01.240 --> 00:18:06.400
I know what you're thinking - what about the eel?

00:18:05.680 --> 00:18:17.760
Well, one day David dived down in search of his beloved corals and what he found changed the way that he looked at the ocean forever.

00:18:17.000 --> 00:18:23.720
So we're making an exhibit for the American Museum of Natural History.

00:18:22.920 --> 00:18:26.400
It's a virtual coral reef.

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:34.040
While we're shooting the night coral reef... something happened that totally changed the trajectory of my research.

00:18:33.200 --> 00:18:42.480
We come back to the lab and in the photos is this one green fluorescent eel.

00:18:41.920 --> 00:18:43.080
Like, unbelievable.

00:18:42.160 --> 00:18:47.960
This is the first green fluorescent fish that we've seen.

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:48.560
And there it was.

00:18:48.080 --> 00:18:50.360
Photo-bombing us.

00:18:50.080 --> 00:18:56.960
This was the first time that a fish had ever been seen fluorescing.

00:18:56.160 --> 00:18:59.200
So was it a one-off?

00:18:58.360 --> 00:19:00.080
A freak of nature?

00:18:59.320 --> 00:19:02.480
Or are there more glowing fish in the sea?

00:19:01.680 --> 00:19:04.880
David had to find out.

00:19:05.960 --> 00:19:12.160
So we designed a new set of big blue lights.

00:19:11.320 --> 00:19:14.800
And we began scouring the reef.

00:19:13.840 --> 00:19:16.360
It's kind of like ET phone home.

00:19:15.400 --> 00:19:24.360
We're giving blue light off and we're waiting for an animal that's going to respond back, shine it back to us.

00:19:23.720 --> 00:19:36.560
And shining back at him was a deep-sea fluorescent wonderland of nearly 200 species of biofluorescent fish.

00:19:35.760 --> 00:19:41.960
Lizard fish, bream, scorpion fish, a whole gamut.

00:19:41.000 --> 00:19:45.360
But the weirdness didn't stop there.

00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:48.960
Yes, the world's first glow-in-the-dark shark.

00:19:48.320 --> 00:19:50.440
Astounding.

00:19:52.400 --> 00:20:01.600
There's little twinkly stars, there's patterns on the females, they have these rings around their eyes.

00:20:00.760 --> 00:20:06.920
In the deep blue, these sharks are emanating patterns of green.

00:20:05.960 --> 00:20:08.360
And the light show didn't stop there.

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:15.680
One more ocean inhabitant had a colourful secret to give away.

00:20:14.960 --> 00:20:20.040
This time it was a marine turtle.

00:20:19.240 --> 00:20:27.840
The first sign ever that reptiles can biofluoresce too.

00:20:29.960 --> 00:20:38.520
And that, for the moment, is where David's weird biofluorescing marine list stops.

00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:40.160
Incredible.

00:20:39.680 --> 00:20:47.640
The fish had been fluorescing right underneath the scientists' noses.

00:20:46.800 --> 00:20:49.160
So, it's an astonishing discovery, there's no doubt of that.

00:20:48.400 --> 00:20:56.960
But it does beg the question - why were these fish producing such a vibrant range of colours?

00:20:56.200 --> 00:21:02.000
It could be used for mating, for courtship.

00:21:01.120 --> 00:21:05.960
It could be used for predator avoidance.

00:21:05.080 --> 00:21:06.800
It's a perfect camouflage for them.

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:17.840
In the swellsharks, it's secret patterns among males and females, perhaps to allow them to separate the sexes, for identification of other members of the same species.

00:21:16.960 --> 00:21:22.360
So it's like a secret channel of communication.

00:21:21.560 --> 00:21:22.680
I like that.

00:21:21.760 --> 00:21:23.640
I like that very much.

00:21:22.920 --> 00:21:32.760
I like the fact that marine life has been communicating using a secret spectrum of colour, something that we're entirely blind to.

00:21:32.160 --> 00:21:37.760
Now, if only we could figure out what they were saying to one another.

00:21:40.040 --> 00:21:47.880
Well, as it happens, our oceans are full of colourful communicators.

00:21:46.960 --> 00:21:53.880
So here we're staying to meet our next magical marvel.

00:21:54.520 --> 00:21:57.080
These little gems.

00:21:56.200 --> 00:22:02.640
The jewel in the crown when it comes to using light for deception.

00:22:02.760 --> 00:22:10.320
Now you see me... now you don't.

00:22:09.440 --> 00:22:15.760
This sparkling blue beauty has been nicknamed a sea sapphire.

00:22:14.800 --> 00:22:17.280
For obvious reasons.

00:22:17.400 --> 00:22:21.160
They measure just a few millimetres.

00:22:20.360 --> 00:22:25.760
They're like the bugs of our oceans - floating around until they get gobbled up by passing fish.

00:22:26.960 --> 00:22:42.720
How remarkable that this tiny, insignificant and yet very, very beautiful little invertebrate has pulled off the Holy Grail of science.

00:22:41.880 --> 00:22:45.320
The ultimate in trickery.

00:22:44.440 --> 00:22:46.720
The art of invisibility.

00:22:46.200 --> 00:22:49.480
But a real life invisibility cloak?

00:22:48.560 --> 00:22:52.120
Surely that's impossible.

00:22:51.360 --> 00:22:55.800
Isn't it?

00:22:54.960 --> 00:23:02.400
It's a question that many a marine biologist has asked themselves.

00:23:01.560 --> 00:23:07.360
You can have one right in front of you in a bowl and you just can't even find it.

00:23:07.160 --> 00:23:08.240
A biologist losing his own subject?

00:23:07.320 --> 00:23:13.280
Come on then, Steve, let us in on its secret.

00:23:12.480 --> 00:23:20.600
Sea sapphires have a unique morphology in that they're very flattened.

00:23:19.640 --> 00:23:21.080
They have very little tissue when you're looking through them.

00:23:20.320 --> 00:23:24.040
The effect, as you might expect, is that it makes them transparent.

00:23:23.400 --> 00:23:29.800
Being thin and translucent clearly helps.

00:23:29.080 --> 00:23:31.000
But there's a little more to it than that.

00:23:30.240 --> 00:23:38.560
They are, in fact, cleverly constructed to get a little helping hand from the sun.

00:23:38.920 --> 00:23:49.440
The surface of the sea sapphire is made of layers of perfectly hexagonal crystals.

00:23:48.600 --> 00:23:49.960
Not just one layer, but several.

00:23:49.200 --> 00:23:50.560
One on top of the other.

00:23:49.800 --> 00:23:55.200
With liquid in between.

00:23:54.360 --> 00:23:55.440
Think of it as like a wafer biscuit.

00:23:55.240 --> 00:24:04.400
As the sea sapphire moves in the water, the blue ocean light gets bounced off of these crystals, creating an iridescent sheen of blues and violets.

00:24:03.720 --> 00:24:09.400
What's more, it can actually move the crystal layers to change its colour.

00:24:09.840 --> 00:24:13.760
A bit like a chameleon.

00:24:13.280 --> 00:24:19.000
The key to its invisibility though is its angle to the sun.

00:24:21.040 --> 00:24:32.920
When the sea sapphire hits a crucial point of 45 degrees to the light the clever crystal layers bounce back only ultraviolet light.

00:24:33.280 --> 00:24:34.480
UV.

00:24:33.560 --> 00:24:36.480
Which our eyes simply can't see.

00:24:39.000 --> 00:24:50.520
Combined with its ultra-thin transparency, the sea sapphire seemingly disappears.

00:24:49.720 --> 00:24:53.280
Now that is one clever trick of the light.

00:24:54.200 --> 00:25:05.080
Having this ability to effectively switch their colour on and off, like a flashing ocean beacon, is what gives these tiny jewels their real advantage.

00:25:04.720 --> 00:25:09.240
The sea sapphires might be using it to find each other.

00:25:08.320 --> 00:25:16.280
You'll see a little column of these guys stacked up, swimming just above each other, all swimming in a little line.

00:25:15.480 --> 00:25:26.600
So they seem to be homing in using this blue iridescence to signal each other in some kind of a social structure.

00:25:26.080 --> 00:25:33.120
That has to be one of the most remarkable organisms that I've ever...

00:25:32.720 --> 00:25:43.640
Using its crystal shimmer to communicate across the vast oceans.

00:25:42.920 --> 00:25:44.240
It's brilliant.

00:25:43.320 --> 00:25:45.360
Absolutely brilliant.

00:25:44.400 --> 00:25:49.560
But I'm still struck by that invisibility.

00:25:52.080 --> 00:25:53.760
That's one superpower I would very much like to have.

00:25:53.000 --> 00:25:59.880
The mind-bending powers of light will never cease to amaze.

00:26:00.840 --> 00:26:05.080
But that is just the tip of nature's iceberg of weirdness.

00:26:04.360 --> 00:26:13.680
Because next, we're headed on a journey of exploration of, well, there's no other way to put it, strange sticky things.

00:26:16.240 --> 00:26:18.880
And it all starts with this guy here.

00:26:18.120 --> 00:26:20.880
Jamie.

00:26:20.080 --> 00:26:23.120
He looks pretty ordinary.

00:26:22.200 --> 00:26:28.640
But in fact, he has an extraordinary superpower.

00:26:27.880 --> 00:26:35.440
My ability is actually sticking anything solid to my body.

00:26:34.480 --> 00:26:42.800
I can stick cans, bottles, cellphones, almost anything to my skin, without no glue.

00:26:41.920 --> 00:26:43.280
(Go on. )

00:26:45.120 --> 00:26:48.440
You know you want to.

00:26:47.520 --> 00:26:55.080
At this point, you want to rush into the kitchen, empty a can, and try and stick it to some part of your body.

00:26:54.280 --> 00:26:55.680
Here.

00:26:54.920 --> 00:26:59.200
I know what you're thinking.

00:26:58.320 --> 00:27:02.280
This simply cannot be true.

00:27:01.400 --> 00:27:02.560
Can it?

00:27:01.720 --> 00:27:04.560
Well, yes, it can.

00:27:03.840 --> 00:27:15.760
Because Jamie holds the catchily titled World Record for most drinks cans placed on the head using air suction.

00:27:14.960 --> 00:27:19.680
And now he has a job as a walking ad campaign.

00:27:20.400 --> 00:27:24.200
So, not only does it all stick to my head...

00:27:25.160 --> 00:27:27.320
Let me put these over here so it's out of the way.

00:27:26.480 --> 00:27:35.440
..It also can stick to my hands, my back, my butt, my legs, my knees, everywhere sticks.

00:27:36.640 --> 00:27:42.760
I discovered my unique talents around seven years old.

00:27:41.840 --> 00:27:44.200
Things started sticking to me.

00:27:43.280 --> 00:27:50.200
I climbed trees, you know, normal boy things, just climbing around.

00:27:49.360 --> 00:27:55.080
That's why I thought it maybe had something to do with the sap from the trees.

00:27:54.240 --> 00:28:04.480
But once he grew out of tree-climbing his incredible ability to, er, stick stuff to himself continued.

00:28:05.440 --> 00:28:06.680
And he knew something was up.

00:28:05.720 --> 00:28:06.960
Wow.

00:28:06.040 --> 00:28:08.760
That's insane.

00:28:10.280 --> 00:28:15.640
I bet that comes in handy when you're getting snacks out of the kitchen fridge.

00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:16.360
Oh, yeah.

00:28:16.360 --> 00:28:19.920
Drive hands-free when you drive.

00:28:18.960 --> 00:28:20.040
Dang.

00:28:19.160 --> 00:28:24.360
These nuts are on your head.

00:28:23.920 --> 00:28:26.680
I'm going to hurt you.

00:28:25.720 --> 00:28:28.680
See?

00:28:27.880 --> 00:28:29.240
Oh, my God.

00:28:28.440 --> 00:28:32.760
So what makes Jamie sticky?

00:28:31.800 --> 00:28:33.280
Everyone's got an opinion, it seems.

00:28:33.400 --> 00:28:40.760
I thought maybe he had a magnetic plate in his head or something.

00:28:39.920 --> 00:28:41.120
You don't have a plate in your head, do you?

00:28:40.280 --> 00:28:42.080
No.

00:28:41.320 --> 00:28:45.240
It's plastic and aluminium.

00:28:44.480 --> 00:28:47.080
None of it can be magnetised.

00:28:46.200 --> 00:28:48.160
Wow.

00:28:47.480 --> 00:28:50.360
So he's not magnetic.

00:28:49.640 --> 00:28:55.360
What he is... is baffling his doctors.

00:28:54.640 --> 00:28:57.480
Lot of doctors have different opinions.

00:28:56.560 --> 00:29:02.680
They actually think I'm a real-life mutant.

00:29:01.880 --> 00:29:06.240
One thinks I'm like a human gecko.

00:29:05.360 --> 00:29:09.920
Another says octopus.

00:29:09.160 --> 00:29:11.920
OK, let's just hold it right there.

00:29:11.160 --> 00:29:14.560
A human gecko?

00:29:13.760 --> 00:29:15.120
Could that really be?

00:29:14.200 --> 00:29:22.080
Geckos stick to things because their bulbous toes are covered in hundreds of microscopic hairs.

00:29:21.560 --> 00:29:32.040
Particles in these bristles interact with particles on the surface of the wall, or tree, creating an electromagnetic attraction.

00:29:31.240 --> 00:29:40.200
Interesting theory for Jamie, but I have to say, he doesn't look particularly hairy to me.

00:29:39.960 --> 00:29:42.840
So what about the octopus theory?

00:29:42.560 --> 00:29:44.640
Octopuses stick using suction.

00:29:43.800 --> 00:29:51.000
Morphing their suckers to the shape of different surfaces.

00:29:50.200 --> 00:29:57.640
Muscles inside the sucker contract to create a watertight, or airtight, seal.

00:29:57.360 --> 00:30:02.120
It almost looks as if I could see the muscles doing something.

00:30:04.440 --> 00:30:06.960
And there's no hiding the suction marks either.

00:30:06.160 --> 00:30:13.880
Perhaps he really is more octopus than human.

00:30:13.200 --> 00:30:17.200
Humans with animal traits.

00:30:16.520 --> 00:30:18.080
I like that.

00:30:17.480 --> 00:30:18.960
I really do.

00:30:18.120 --> 00:30:23.280
But there is a human trait here that we didn't want to overlook.

00:30:22.480 --> 00:30:30.080
Have you noticed that Jamie seems to be extraordinarily sweaty?

00:30:30.440 --> 00:30:39.080
Because my body temperature is so hot, usually when it's really warm I have to constantly dry my head off cos the sweat clogs my pores.

00:30:38.200 --> 00:30:42.480
I think he has some sort of perspiration on his head that sticks that stuff.

00:30:41.600 --> 00:30:43.440
It's amazing.

00:30:42.520 --> 00:30:43.560
Yeah.

00:30:44.560 --> 00:30:50.600
So could human sweat be stick enough to make things stick?

00:30:49.800 --> 00:30:55.800
We asked a dermatologist to take a look at the evidence.

00:30:54.880 --> 00:30:57.080
It's remarkable.

00:30:57.920 --> 00:31:05.360
They seem to be pretty solidly stuck.

00:31:05.000 --> 00:31:08.400
I've never seen anything like Jamie before.

00:31:07.600 --> 00:31:12.640
His skin looks normal but he's sweating a bit.

00:31:11.880 --> 00:31:15.720
The majority of sweat is just straightforward water.

00:31:14.880 --> 00:31:25.000
As the water evaporates it leaves behind the sebum, and that's the oily substance, our skin will end up feeling a little bit sticky.

00:31:24.120 --> 00:31:31.160
Some people complain about excess sweating and that's a recognised condition.

00:31:30.320 --> 00:31:36.520
I've never come across any condition of the skin which specifically makes it more sticky.

00:31:35.960 --> 00:31:40.160
But the sweat may be important nonetheless.

00:31:40.440 --> 00:31:48.800
Just from the video footage, I would say that this is a suction thing rather than purely a sticky thing.

00:31:48.920 --> 00:31:55.480
But if the skin was completely dry it would be much more difficult to create a good contact suction between the can and the skin.

00:31:55.040 --> 00:31:58.360
So a little bit of moisture there is probably helpful for this.

00:31:58.200 --> 00:32:02.000
It's an amazing talent, the way he does it.

00:32:03.400 --> 00:32:11.120
Suction alone, however, doesn't explain how he can stick a mobile to his face.

00:32:10.280 --> 00:32:15.560
So, Jamie's condition still leaves rather more questions than answers.

00:32:14.920 --> 00:32:19.960
But whatever the reason turns out to be, for Jamie, it seems it's a skill that has its perks.

00:32:20.600 --> 00:32:23.600
I actually do love having this ability.

00:32:23.160 --> 00:32:25.920
It puts a smile on people's faces.

00:32:25.000 --> 00:32:29.280
They just look at me and they start laughing.

00:32:28.320 --> 00:32:31.920
Even if they think I'm a goof or whatever.

00:32:31.160 --> 00:32:32.400
Why would anyone think that(?)

00:32:33.880 --> 00:32:42.120
Jamie - also known as Canhead - his name, not mine, I'm not being rude - is still mostly a mystery.

00:32:41.480 --> 00:32:45.800
But it has also made him somewhat of a celebrity.

00:32:45.400 --> 00:32:53.480
He now makes thousands of dollars wandering around America sticking cans to his head.

00:32:53.040 --> 00:32:57.600
Something to aspire to, I suppose.

00:32:58.520 --> 00:33:01.280
Jamie's sticky superpower is just a bit of fun.

00:33:04.840 --> 00:33:13.000
But for one Australian resident, sticking things on its head is a matter of life or death.

00:33:12.880 --> 00:33:14.120
That resident is a caterpillar.

00:33:14.880 --> 00:33:18.520
But no ordinary caterpillar.

00:33:17.680 --> 00:33:21.040
A very strange one indeed.

00:33:24.080 --> 00:33:32.920
But to fully understand it, you have at first got to get to grips with just how difficult it is to be a caterpillar.

00:33:33.440 --> 00:33:36.160
Basically, their lives are pretty awful.

00:33:36.480 --> 00:33:46.600
They spend most of their life trying to avoid being eaten by various things.

00:33:46.920 --> 00:33:54.680
If you're a caterpillar, birds, mammals, reptiles, even other insects think you're delicious.

00:33:53.720 --> 00:34:00.960
In the UK, a young blue tit can eat over 100 caterpillars a day.

00:34:00.080 --> 00:34:09.000
Oh, and parasitic wasps like to lay their young inside you so that one day they'll explode out of your insides, killing you in the process.

00:34:08.040 --> 00:34:12.159
So what is a poor little caterpillar to do?

00:34:14.280 --> 00:34:21.360
Well, one option could be to pile some spare heads on top of each other.

00:34:21.280 --> 00:34:23.120
As protection.

00:34:22.400 --> 00:34:23.719
It's obvious, really.

00:34:25.520 --> 00:34:29.920
Yes, I did just say "spare heads."

00:34:28.960 --> 00:34:33.560
And yes, that is in fact possible.

00:34:32.600 --> 00:34:36.199
Meet the world's maddest caterpillar.

00:34:35.360 --> 00:34:39.639
A caterpillar with a hat made of heads.

00:34:38.840 --> 00:34:43.080
A Mad Hatterpillar, if you will.

00:34:43.600 --> 00:34:49.360
Otherwise known as a gum leaf skeletoniser.

00:34:48.600 --> 00:34:52.120
It's a common Australian pest.

00:34:51.239 --> 00:34:52.400
With a remarkable appendage.

00:34:53.880 --> 00:34:57.159
So, how did this come to be?

00:34:57.520 --> 00:35:06.840
One of the challenges of being an insect is that to grow you need to shed your hard outer exoskeleton and get yourself a newer, bigger one that you can fill up from the inside.

00:35:06.120 --> 00:35:12.200
When you do that, you get a chance to reinvent yourself with a brand-new look.

00:35:11.320 --> 00:35:12.640
That's one way to put it.

00:35:11.760 --> 00:35:19.880
Normally caterpillars eat the skin they shed to recycle the nutrients within.

00:35:18.920 --> 00:35:23.240
Not our Mad Hatterpillar though.

00:35:22.280 --> 00:35:24.360
It keeps its old heads.

00:35:24.160 --> 00:35:27.480
It stacks them on top of its head like some sort of bizarre insect unicorn.

00:35:26.560 --> 00:35:36.240
So it's got the bigger heads at the bottom, the smaller, older, used heads up the top, and it forms this great tower of old head capsules on top of its head.

00:35:35.480 --> 00:35:41.440
Interlocking hairs and tiny hooks keep the heads firmly stuck in place.

00:35:40.480 --> 00:35:41.640
Most of the time.

00:35:40.800 --> 00:35:44.520
When I first saw them, you just look at them and think, "What are you doing?

00:35:44.440 --> 00:35:50.800
"What is the possible benefit for having all those old heads "stuck on top of your new head?"

00:35:51.200 --> 00:35:55.680
Well, that, Dieter, is a good question.

00:35:54.760 --> 00:35:57.280
Why indeed?

00:35:57.040 --> 00:36:00.200
Dieter knew it was for protection, but just how?

00:35:59.320 --> 00:36:01.160
He had to find out.

00:36:02.200 --> 00:36:10.760
He put a Mad Hatterpillar with an impressive headdress in a Petri dish with its nemesis, the stink bug.

00:36:09.840 --> 00:36:19.400
A stink bug is an insect with a needle-like mouth part that stabs its prey, injects it with toxins, and then sucks it dry.

00:36:19.600 --> 00:36:24.600
Definitely something to avoid.

00:36:23.880 --> 00:36:25.920
And here is what happened.

00:36:25.120 --> 00:36:28.000
What we found is the caterpillars are actually physically deflecting attacks by some of the predators.

00:36:28.360 --> 00:36:38.560
The wobbling of the head or the really dramatic shaking of the head like you're using a sword to try and keep the animal away from you.

00:36:41.840 --> 00:36:46.320
The predators were also sometimes attacking his stack of head capsules instead of the actual body of the caterpillar.

00:36:45.440 --> 00:36:50.960
And sometimes all it takes is that one chance to get away... to make you win or lose that bout.

00:36:50.920 --> 00:36:51.960
Don't get too excited.

00:36:51.120 --> 00:36:53.320
Most of the caterpillars lost the bouts, regardless.

00:36:53.880 --> 00:37:03.960
But when Dieter tested the theory in the big, wide world outside the Petri dish, he got some interesting results.

00:37:04.480 --> 00:37:14.640
When the animals were in groups where some had head capsules and others didn't, the ones without them were far more likely to get eaten than the ones that hadn't.

00:37:15.840 --> 00:37:24.160
In other words, caterpillars that did have head stacks, living amongst those that didn't, survived better than their headless counterparts.

00:37:23.480 --> 00:37:33.280
So the moral of that story is, I suppose, if you're a Mad Hatterpillar with a towering head stack, stick near your mates with only one head.

00:37:33.720 --> 00:37:41.240
That way, when an enemy approaches, your mate will make far easier pickings than you.

00:37:40.360 --> 00:37:47.040
It certainly gives meaning to the phrase "many heads are better than one."

00:37:46.200 --> 00:37:49.360
So, all hail the power of the sticky.

00:37:48.400 --> 00:37:49.640
An enviable superpower if ever there was one.

00:37:58.880 --> 00:38:07.720
Finally, we're going to introduce you to a series of animals whose weird behaviour leaves a lot to be desired.

00:38:09.840 --> 00:38:14.120
Kicking us off, a refreshing dip in the west Irish Sea.

00:38:14.360 --> 00:38:20.240
A hot summer's day off the coast of Doolin.

00:38:19.720 --> 00:38:23.000
Perfect for a swim.

00:38:22.160 --> 00:38:28.720
But when the locals hit the surf, they found they had company.

00:38:28.280 --> 00:38:35.400
A sociable, affable dolphin, and not just any old dolphin, it was Dusty the dolphin.

00:38:34.480 --> 00:38:43.320
A female bottlenose who'd become quite a celebrity on the Irish coast.

00:38:42.880 --> 00:38:50.400
Dusty is one of Ireland's friendly, solitary, community dolphins.

00:38:49.480 --> 00:38:55.040
Like famous Fungie of Dingle.

00:38:56.760 --> 00:39:05.320
Dusty's story starts some 16 years ago when she first appeared here as an adolescent.

00:39:04.640 --> 00:39:08.680
And never left.

00:39:07.720 --> 00:39:14.840
Hanging out with the locals year after year after year.

00:39:14.200 --> 00:39:17.520
Building up quite the CV.

00:39:16.680 --> 00:39:17.960
There she is.

00:39:17.000 --> 00:39:18.440
Dusty.

00:39:17.480 --> 00:39:18.760
Hello.

00:39:17.800 --> 00:39:19.600
Hello.

00:39:19.200 --> 00:39:22.000
Dusty the peacekeeper.

00:39:23.280 --> 00:39:25.920
She's brought so much joy to so many people.

00:39:25.080 --> 00:39:28.360
She induces a lot of peace and...

00:39:27.560 --> 00:39:30.880
I guess, love.

00:39:30.800 --> 00:39:31.960
You know?

00:39:31.000 --> 00:39:35.240
Dusty the environmentalist.

00:39:34.760 --> 00:39:40.080
She came and she had the huge white bag on her nose.

00:39:39.120 --> 00:39:40.600
And she brought it to us.

00:39:39.720 --> 00:39:44.400
She was playing games but cleaning the ocean.

00:39:43.440 --> 00:39:45.160
It was so beautiful to do it with her.

00:39:49.360 --> 00:39:52.480
And Dusty the life-saver.

00:39:51.680 --> 00:39:55.200
Yes, Dusty helped saved surfer Luke's life.

00:39:54.240 --> 00:39:59.880
Guiding him back to the shore after his surfboard split in two.

00:39:58.920 --> 00:40:07.960
Everybody said that it was an amazing thing to watch - a dolphin sort of shepherd somebody in.

00:40:07.400 --> 00:40:10.040
Especially someone who's struggling.

00:40:09.160 --> 00:40:12.520
It was a very beautiful experience.

00:40:11.600 --> 00:40:13.000
St Dusty.

00:40:15.280 --> 00:40:22.760
She's swum with local residents Kate and George for so long she's become part of the family.

00:40:25.880 --> 00:40:29.280
It's no wonder when people see scenes like this that they want to join in.

00:40:31.000 --> 00:40:36.680
Which is what local resident Valerie did in July 2013.

00:40:35.920 --> 00:40:39.400
But she was in for a surprise.

00:40:38.560 --> 00:40:42.320
Dusty, it seemed, had gone mad.

00:40:46.840 --> 00:40:54.040
I knew I needed to get out of the water quick.

00:40:53.120 --> 00:40:58.600
I went to swim out but it slammed into me.

00:40:57.800 --> 00:41:01.560
It was very powerful and she hit me with her nose.

00:41:00.640 --> 00:41:07.560
I had six spinal fractures, some broken ribs and a damaged lung.

00:41:14.040 --> 00:41:27.520
In fact, this is not the only time this five-metre mammal has launched itself at swimmers.

00:41:30.120 --> 00:41:34.400
So how do you reconcile these two images?

00:41:33.600 --> 00:41:45.480
Well, to understand, we need to get to grips with what sets Dusty apart from other dolphins.

00:41:44.680 --> 00:41:54.480
Now we all know that dolphins are highly social animals, living together in pods.

00:41:53.640 --> 00:41:58.160
So what on earth was she doing swimming around on her own?

00:41:57.960 --> 00:42:01.960
Solitary dolphins are common amongst males.

00:42:01.160 --> 00:42:10.440
Adolescent males hang out alone, getting big and strong so they can compete for access to females.

00:42:09.600 --> 00:42:10.640
But Dusty is a female.

00:42:14.960 --> 00:42:19.000
It's a bit weird, to be honest.

00:42:18.200 --> 00:42:22.120
And it's not typical or normal of bottlenose dolphins.

00:42:21.400 --> 00:42:26.480
When there's other dolphins around she almost seems to be hiding from them.

00:42:25.560 --> 00:42:33.600
It seems to be that, in Ireland especially, people are almost looking for dolphins to swim with.

00:42:33.080 --> 00:42:37.600
You know, to go and befriend.

00:42:37.840 --> 00:42:41.720
So I'm not convinced the dolphin is seeking out humans.

00:42:40.760 --> 00:42:46.320
I'm not convinced they're choosing a solitary existence.

00:42:47.480 --> 00:42:54.160
Whether she chose us or we chose her, there's plenty of dolphin left in Dusty.

00:42:53.360 --> 00:42:54.640
She's just rolled.

00:42:53.680 --> 00:42:57.520
And dolphins need other dolphins.

00:42:56.800 --> 00:43:05.080
It's possible that Dusty now sees her human "friends" as her surrogate dolphin pod.

00:43:04.160 --> 00:43:07.720
And that could begin to explain the aggression.

00:43:07.000 --> 00:43:16.960
If Dusty treats humans like dolphins treat dolphins, well, put it this way, bottlenose dolphins can be quite brutal.

00:43:19.320 --> 00:43:24.520
We perceive dolphins as these friendly animals.

00:43:23.560 --> 00:43:25.560
They have that lovely smiley face.

00:43:24.760 --> 00:43:29.280
You know, don't trust everything that smiles.

00:43:28.600 --> 00:43:30.600
It could be the smile of a sadistic killer dolphin.

00:43:30.280 --> 00:43:38.800
This is a highly-evolved marine mammal that is strong, can wipe you out with a flick of the tail.

00:43:38.240 --> 00:43:40.120
Ram you, which is what bottlenose dolphins do.

00:43:39.880 --> 00:43:46.680
They're covered in scratches and scars, they're covered in notches, they're covered in tooth rakes.

00:43:45.880 --> 00:43:50.440
The trouble is, humans are not built like dolphins.

00:43:49.560 --> 00:43:50.800
We don't just come away with scars.

00:43:49.960 --> 00:43:57.000
We come away literally broken.

00:44:00.760 --> 00:44:04.160
But Dusty only gets mad with certain people.

00:44:03.400 --> 00:44:07.840
So, what about her Jekyll and Hyde character?

00:44:07.160 --> 00:44:15.840
Why do some people get darling Dusty and others, a very angry dolphin?

00:44:15.040 --> 00:44:22.000
It all depends, it seems, on whether or not you're in her pod.

00:44:21.120 --> 00:44:27.640
If you're a regular to Dusty, like Kate and George, you're fine.

00:44:26.800 --> 00:44:29.720
But if you're a new face, like Valerie, you're a threat.

00:44:28.800 --> 00:44:40.600
I've learned, since my accident, that when Dusty's having a moment with people who do swim with her, she doesn't like to be interrupted.

00:44:39.640 --> 00:44:42.800
She gets very territorial.

00:44:41.840 --> 00:44:45.560
And I think that's the reason why she attacked me.

00:44:46.920 --> 00:44:53.280
Sadly for Valerie, she just wasn't in the in-crowd.

00:44:52.600 --> 00:44:55.440
She accepts some people and she doesn't accept some people.

00:44:54.800 --> 00:44:56.960
It's hard, but it's like that.

00:44:56.040 --> 00:44:58.160
Definitely.

00:44:57.360 --> 00:44:58.880
It's like that.

00:45:00.120 --> 00:45:01.360
Valerie was lucky.

00:45:00.400 --> 00:45:01.520
She recovered from her attack.

00:45:00.680 --> 00:45:05.840
And what's more, she's made peace with Dusty.

00:45:05.600 --> 00:45:08.560
After a while she came back to me.

00:45:07.600 --> 00:45:10.320
She was kind of bobbing vertically.

00:45:09.360 --> 00:45:14.200
And we just locked eyes.

00:45:13.360 --> 00:45:19.600
I personally felt there was a lot of remorse and she was trying to apologise.

00:45:18.720 --> 00:45:21.080
So I don't have any problem with Dusty at all.

00:45:20.200 --> 00:45:23.840
But I wouldn't get in the water, no.

00:45:25.760 --> 00:45:29.000
Dolphins are not like humans.

00:45:28.080 --> 00:45:32.280
They are wild.

00:45:31.400 --> 00:45:32.920
And unpredictable.

00:45:32.120 --> 00:45:37.880
So don't get confused by the smile.

00:45:40.520 --> 00:45:48.480
But with man and beast living alongside each other, it's perhaps inevitable that we expect them to behave like us.

00:45:48.520 --> 00:45:50.040
Like our next colourful character.

00:45:49.200 --> 00:45:52.280
A delinquent down under.

00:45:51.400 --> 00:45:55.240
This is a lorikeet.

00:45:54.680 --> 00:46:00.760
It's a common Australian bird, likely to cross your path.

00:45:59.920 --> 00:46:10.040
But if you were to spot one in the small town of Palmerston, near Darwin, it's highly likely it would look like this.

00:46:09.160 --> 00:46:14.000
Polly parrot is having a bad day.

00:46:16.320 --> 00:46:26.400
Frankly, she looks like she's had one too many down the local pub.

00:46:25.480 --> 00:46:36.440
Every year, the rescue centre here in Palmerston fills up with, apparently, pickled parrots.

00:46:36.160 --> 00:46:40.720
And every year, the birds' antics hit the headlines.

00:46:39.760 --> 00:46:49.800
'About 200 lorikeets are brought to the hospital suffering the 'ill effects of what appears to be a big night out.'

00:46:49.120 --> 00:46:53.960
It's not my fault you've been drinking.

00:46:53.640 --> 00:46:59.120
For decades, these lorikeets have been mocked for their drunken antics.

00:46:58.240 --> 00:47:02.880
The story of these boozing birds is ingrained in local folklore.

00:47:03.200 --> 00:47:07.320
They wobble about.

00:47:06.440 --> 00:47:07.880
Their balance is affected.

00:47:07.120 --> 00:47:10.400
They're weak and they stagger.

00:47:11.000 --> 00:47:19.200
It's pretty much the same as what happens to people when they're very drunk and fall over and hurt themselves.

00:47:20.760 --> 00:47:25.360
Now, obviously, these birds haven't been drinking beer.

00:47:24.440 --> 00:47:29.760
They don't have hands, they couldn't raise a glass, could they?

00:47:29.080 --> 00:47:33.040
But that doesn't mean we should dismiss this as a silly story.

00:47:32.240 --> 00:47:37.560
You see, their inebriated state is certainly cause for concern.

00:47:39.040 --> 00:47:48.320
This strange behaviour only occurs in the wet season.

00:47:47.640 --> 00:47:55.720
So why is this one species, once a year, behaving so oddly?

00:47:54.920 --> 00:48:03.800
Well, the blame always lands firmly at the roots of what is known as the drunken parrot tree.

00:48:03.280 --> 00:48:07.240
It's laden with sweet, tasty nectar.

00:48:06.280 --> 00:48:09.320
It's believed though that as the year hots up, the nectar ferments.

00:48:10.120 --> 00:48:25.760
All sweet things need to ferment is a bit of heat, a bit of moisture, and some natural yeast... that eats the sugars and converts them to ethanol, or alcohol.

00:48:24.920 --> 00:48:35.320
And, so says the local legend, as the birds feed on the fermented nectar they get drunk.

00:48:34.400 --> 00:48:35.720
It's not a crazy theory.

00:48:34.880 --> 00:48:40.400
Fermenting fruit can cause drunk-like behaviour in many other animals.

00:48:39.440 --> 00:48:44.320
A squirrel after one too many fermented pumpkins.

00:48:44.440 --> 00:48:52.520
And the Swedish moose that overdid the fermented apples.

00:48:51.600 --> 00:48:57.040
But the lorikeets aren't just a bit tipsy.

00:48:56.760 --> 00:49:03.240
No, they take weeks to recover... in hospital.

00:49:02.320 --> 00:49:08.680
So were these birds getting drunk on fermenting fruit and nectar in the same way that that Swedish moose was?

00:49:08.120 --> 00:49:10.040
Well, no.

00:49:09.360 --> 00:49:15.160
In the small Australian town of Palmerston, things were altogether more worrying.

00:49:14.320 --> 00:49:22.360
I've been told they recently did some blood tests on some of these lorikeets and they found no evidence of ethanol.

00:49:21.600 --> 00:49:28.840
No ethanol means no alcohol.

00:49:28.400 --> 00:49:32.720
So, definitively, they're not drunk.

00:49:31.840 --> 00:49:37.200
Could the locals' firmly-held belief be about to be turned on its head?

00:49:36.640 --> 00:49:38.960
Well, yes.

00:49:38.200 --> 00:49:40.120
It's recently been found that what's attacking their body is most likely a virus.

00:49:41.160 --> 00:49:47.680
Like alcohol, viruses can interfere with how the brain works.

00:49:46.720 --> 00:49:53.480
Affecting vision, balance and coordination.

00:49:52.720 --> 00:49:57.360
In other words, causing similar symptoms to drunkenness.

00:49:57.080 --> 00:50:04.120
You can see people who are suffering from influenza are very weak, they're hot, they fall around, they get headaches.

00:50:03.240 --> 00:50:08.280
Pretty much like when they're drunk.

00:50:08.040 --> 00:50:13.240
Just imagine how bad it would feel to have flu in mid-air.

00:50:12.280 --> 00:50:17.080
I think they're probably trying to fly and they just run out of breath.

00:50:16.160 --> 00:50:17.960
They faint in mid-air and fall to the ground.

00:50:18.080 --> 00:50:19.120
Dramatic stuff.

00:50:20.800 --> 00:50:25.600
And why does it only happen in the wet season?

00:50:24.960 --> 00:50:26.320
Well, that's most likely down to these pests.

00:50:25.520 --> 00:50:30.880
Mosquitoes and midges.

00:50:29.960 --> 00:50:38.400
Famously partial to hot, steamy weather and famously disease-ridden too.

00:50:37.440 --> 00:50:42.040
It's huge news for Palmerston's misunderstood parrots.

00:50:41.240 --> 00:50:43.520
No more character assassination.

00:50:43.560 --> 00:50:47.240
The truth is finally out.

00:50:46.760 --> 00:50:52.640
Far from being drunk, these unlucky lorikeets are, in fact, seriously ill.

00:50:52.400 --> 00:51:01.040
Thankfully, with the right care, most can be back in the wild within a few weeks.

00:51:00.080 --> 00:51:05.000
But it seems that happy hour is not so happy after all.

00:51:04.160 --> 00:51:06.520
At least for the birds.

00:51:12.080 --> 00:51:17.640
Our final weird wonder is famous worldwide.

00:51:16.920 --> 00:51:19.320
Well, amongst birders like me, anyway.

00:51:18.680 --> 00:51:31.240
It's a bird whose behaviour is so mysterious that it outwitted scientists in a game of hide-and-seek that lasted for over 100 years.

00:51:30.440 --> 00:51:37.800
So much so that they began to wonder if it had gone the same way as the dodo.

00:51:36.920 --> 00:51:41.160
It's the Holy Grail of birds for birders.

00:51:40.400 --> 00:51:43.520
Not only in Australia, perhaps in the world.

00:51:42.720 --> 00:51:48.760
It's nominated by the Smithsonian Institute as the world's most mysterious bird.

00:51:47.920 --> 00:51:51.600
And it is the night parrot.

00:51:51.760 --> 00:52:05.080
Common in Australia hundreds of years ago, but around 1912 this reclusive species simply disappeared.

00:52:05.920 --> 00:52:12.800
Rumour and a few museum specimens revealed them to look a bit like big budgerigars.

00:52:11.920 --> 00:52:18.160
A nocturnal, ground-dwelling, ground-nesting parrot.

00:52:17.680 --> 00:52:22.800
Not the best ingredients for survival.

00:52:21.840 --> 00:52:27.320
They hid amongst Australia's arid spinifex grassland.

00:52:26.960 --> 00:52:28.040
Well, supposedly.

00:52:27.280 --> 00:52:31.680
Problem was, no-one could find one.

00:52:31.040 --> 00:52:38.880
So, in 1989, a $25,000 reward was offered for their discovery.

00:52:38.000 --> 00:52:41.400
Dead or alive.

00:52:40.520 --> 00:52:46.040
Everyone took to the road.

00:52:45.080 --> 00:52:53.440
From truck drivers to scientists.

00:52:52.480 --> 00:52:56.080
And what happened next was incredible.

00:52:55.400 --> 00:53:06.240
The curator of ornithology at the Australian Museum was travelling in outback Queensland and stopped by the side of the road, legend has it, to relieve himself.

00:53:05.360 --> 00:53:10.360
As he looked down at his feet, there was the remains of a night parrot.

00:53:09.600 --> 00:53:11.600
A dead night parrot carcass.

00:53:10.840 --> 00:53:20.480
A curator of ornithology had accidentally found a night parrot in the middle of the vast outback.

00:53:19.880 --> 00:53:24.600
The first confirmed sighting in over seven decades.

00:53:24.400 --> 00:53:35.440
Can you just imagine how excited he must have been when he finally rediscovered that bird after all of that time spent looking for it?

00:53:34.760 --> 00:53:37.280
OK, I know it wasn't ideal.

00:53:36.440 --> 00:53:40.040
It was dead.

00:53:39.600 --> 00:53:46.800
But nevertheless, it did prove that this mythical bird wasn't a myth after all.

00:53:45.920 --> 00:53:52.880
And so began the hunt for a living specimen.

00:53:51.920 --> 00:53:54.720
16 more years went by.

00:53:54.760 --> 00:54:00.240
And finally, another was found lying beside a fence.

00:54:00.600 --> 00:54:01.920
But guess what?

00:54:00.960 --> 00:54:02.880
Yes, it was dead too.

00:54:01.920 --> 00:54:09.560
Presumably it had hit the fence and decapitated itself on the fence.

00:54:08.720 --> 00:54:10.720
Unlucky.

00:54:12.640 --> 00:54:20.040
But trust me, patience in ornithology does eventually pay off.

00:54:19.360 --> 00:54:22.880
People were so dedicated and so determined.

00:54:22.000 --> 00:54:26.240
There was a lot of obsession amongst certain people.

00:54:25.360 --> 00:54:29.040
One of those determined obsessives was John Young.

00:54:28.160 --> 00:54:33.600
A naturalist who'd spent 15 years traipsing the outback for clues.

00:54:32.720 --> 00:54:38.040
Finally, in 2013, success.

00:54:37.640 --> 00:54:45.080
A bunch of feathers led him to a live individual.

00:54:45.080 --> 00:54:52.200
After 100 years, the game of hide-and-seek was over.

00:54:52.120 --> 00:54:57.040
And the scientists had won.

00:54:56.200 --> 00:54:59.960
I just can't tell you how exciting this discovery was.

00:54:59.040 --> 00:55:04.240
This was a bird brought back from the dead.

00:55:03.360 --> 00:55:08.560
This was ornithological dynamite.

00:55:07.600 --> 00:55:12.040
The bird-watching equivalent of a supernova.

00:55:11.200 --> 00:55:15.080
But don't go grabbing your binoculars just yet.

00:55:14.280 --> 00:55:21.840
To protect the bird, John kept the exact location a secret.

00:55:21.040 --> 00:55:29.520
And to this day, only three people on the planet are confirmed to have seen live night parrots.

00:55:29.440 --> 00:55:35.840
And this guy here, ecologist Steve Murphy, has gone one better.

00:55:34.960 --> 00:55:36.400
He's actually held one.

00:55:35.560 --> 00:55:42.760
When he and his wife fitted one with a tracker.

00:55:41.920 --> 00:55:44.400
We really had to focus.

00:55:44.240 --> 00:55:48.000
We were both, you know, shaking pretty madly at the start.

00:55:47.520 --> 00:55:52.400
The sense of responsibility was just awesome.

00:55:54.880 --> 00:56:02.960
There is still just one known population of night parrots in the world.

00:56:02.040 --> 00:56:10.040
The rest were wiped out by fire, and their number one predator, yes, kitty.

00:56:09.080 --> 00:56:17.360
There's quite a famous story of the telegraph stationmaster at Alice Springs in the early 1800s.

00:56:16.480 --> 00:56:26.000
Lining the inside of his cabin with the wings of night parrots that had been brought in each night by his pet cat.

00:56:25.080 --> 00:56:33.760
Unfortunately for the cats, the best way to keep this chubby little parrot alive is to take kitty out of the equation.

00:56:32.960 --> 00:56:34.560
Where's the cat?

00:56:33.720 --> 00:56:35.080
Good girl.

00:56:34.120 --> 00:56:35.960
Good girl, Mag.

00:56:35.000 --> 00:56:37.280
And for a while it seemed to be working.

00:56:38.560 --> 00:56:47.360
When, in 2016, Steve Murphy made an incredible discovery.

00:56:46.880 --> 00:56:48.680
A nest!

00:56:47.800 --> 00:56:53.200
A nest of two perfect night parrot eggs.

00:56:52.680 --> 00:56:56.440
This was dynamite.

00:56:55.640 --> 00:57:02.640
This was like finding the pot of gold at the end of the rainbow.

00:57:01.920 --> 00:57:06.360
Not only were night parrots alive, they were breeding.

00:57:05.520 --> 00:57:13.720
But then, just like every other aspect of this legendary tale, there was another twist in the plot.

00:57:12.840 --> 00:57:18.200
You see, a couple of days later, Steve returned to the nest.

00:57:18.040 --> 00:57:22.880
And what did he find when he peered in?

00:57:22.320 --> 00:57:24.400
Broken eggshells.

00:57:23.600 --> 00:57:25.800
Can you believe it?

00:57:25.120 --> 00:57:29.040
Can you imagine how he felt?

00:57:28.080 --> 00:57:41.240
Well, so great is the desire to preserve this species, so enormous was the gravity of this crime that the shells were collected and sent for analysis to try and identify the culprit.

00:57:41.360 --> 00:57:45.760
And this time it was no cat.

00:57:46.760 --> 00:57:52.720
It was a native king brown snake.

00:57:52.120 --> 00:57:59.440
A new player in this now deadly game of hide-and-seek.

00:58:01.480 --> 00:58:03.560
The story of this ground-living bird continues.

00:58:02.720 --> 00:58:13.880
And in fact, due to the incredible secrecy about its location, its mythical status, its legend lives on.

00:58:16.160 --> 00:58:24.720
From devilish dolphins via not so boozy birds to a very private parrot.

00:58:24.600 --> 00:58:36.160
So, whether it's ancient legends or brand-new discoveries, there's no doubt at all that there are plenty of weird stories out there.

00:58:35.560 --> 00:58:40.560
And I can promise you one thing, there are a lot more to come.

00:58:41.280 --> 00:58:43.760
Next time...

00:58:42.840 --> 00:58:46.600
We'll be poking a giant pile of worms...

00:58:45.760 --> 00:58:47.000
I've never ever seen that before.

00:58:46.160 --> 00:58:50.560
..Meeting nature's artistic masterminds...

00:58:49.920 --> 00:58:52.440
I was just like, "Oh, my God!"

00:58:51.880 --> 00:58:59.320
..And solving a sperm whale mystery.

00:59:52.120 --> 00:59:53.200
Celebrate the Starman's last years on Earth with a landmark documentary...

00:59:52.320 --> 00:59:53.440
Panorama investigates the deadly terrorist attack on a Tunisian beach.

00:59:56.600 --> 00:59:59.240
Why wasn't security tighter and should British tourists have been warned about the risks?
